1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:04,283
The game needs one scene that starts
everything.

2
00:00:04,283 --> 00:00:10,746
A new scene called Bootstrap, with a
camera and an event system for button
clicks,

3
00:00:10,746 --> 00:00:14,214
and an empty object for our start-up
script.

4
00:00:20,500 --> 00:00:22,386
Two lines start the UI.

5
00:00:22,386 --> 00:00:25,421
Initialize loads the config we built.

6
00:00:25,421 --> 00:00:27,963
Show State shows the main menu.

7
00:00:27,963 --> 00:00:33,786
That's it: you never put a UI manager in
your scene, it creates itself.

8
00:00:55,500 --> 00:00:56,364
Press Play.

9
00:00:56,364 --> 00:00:59,900
There's our menu, scaling in, with both
bars.

10
00:00:59,900 --> 00:01:04,929
Look at the Hierarchy: each element was
loaded as its own scene.

11
00:01:20,500 --> 00:01:22,231
Now the Store button.

12
00:01:22,231 --> 00:01:25,775
When it's clicked, we show the store
state.

13
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:27,011
One line again.

14
00:01:27,011 --> 00:01:31,214
Drag the button into the field, and save
the scene.

15
00:01:55,500 --> 00:02:02,313
The nav bar does the same with two
buttons: Home shows the menu state, Store
shows

16
00:02:02,313 --> 00:02:03,643
the store state.

17
00:02:15,500 --> 00:02:17,509
Press Play and tap Store.

18
00:02:17,509 --> 00:02:24,018
The store slides in, and the bars stay
exactly where they are: they're shared, so

19
00:02:24,018 --> 00:02:25,464
they never reload.

20
00:02:25,464 --> 00:02:26,589
Home and back.

21
00:02:26,589 --> 00:02:32,214
Scenes load when a state needs them and
unload when nothing uses them.

22
00:02:45,500 --> 00:02:52,149
In a bigger game, send these calls through
one navigator class, as the showcase app

23
00:02:52,149 --> 00:02:52,549
does.

24
00:02:52,549 --> 00:02:54,071
Last step: a popup.
